## AI assistant config (user-specific), EXCEPT the skills
#
# GeneralsX: The skills are tracked, for the same reason is:
# they are not user-specific, they hold the working protocol the sessions depend on, and sessions
# run on Mac, Linux and Windows machines. Ignored, a clone elsewhere has none of it.
#
# THE THREE-LINE SHAPE IS LOAD-BEARING AND TOOK TWO WRONG ATTEMPTS.
#   * Line 2 of this file is `.*`, which excludes the .claude DIRECTORY itself - so it is that
#     rule, not the `/.claude/` one, that was actually hiding these. Re-include the directory
#     first or nothing beneath it is reachable.
#   * Git NEVER DESCENDS into an excluded directory, so a `!` re-include under one can never
#     fire. The contents must be excluded (`/. /*`), not the directory.
# Written either of the wrong ways, the exception is silently dead: `git add` reports "paths are
# ignored", adds nothing, and a commit still lands carrying only this file.
!/.claude/
/.claude/*
!/.claude/skills/
!/.claude/skills/**
# GeneralsX: The SKILLS are not user-specific and are tracked, for the
# same reason below is: they hold the working protocol - which resource a session may
# claim, why a claim is proven both directions, how a handback is typed - and sessions run on
# Mac, Linux and Windows machines. Ignored, a clone elsewhere has none of it.
#
# It also hid them from the gate that exists to catch exactly this. `gx promote` warns about
# untracked work because a file never `git add`ed is absent from the merge candidate - but an
# IGNORED file is invisible to `git status` too, so both new skills sat on disk while the gate
# reported "nothing uncommitted-and-new" and passed. A path that matters must be trackable, or
# no amount of checking will see it.
!/.claude/skills/
!/.claude/skills/**
# GeneralsX: is deliberately NOT ignored any more.
# It was listed here as "user-specific", but this project's copy holds engineering rules the
# work depends on - lockstep limits, atomic four-peer rebuilds, digest path lists, shell traps -
# and sessions run on Mac, Linux and Windows machines. Leaving it untracked means a
# clone on another machine has none of them, which is how a session there rebuilt against the
# wrong assumptions. Personal preferences belong in the operator's own ~/.claude/.

